    "id": "prop:joint-only-region",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "For \\(\\kappa=1/2\\), \\(\\eta=1/10\\), and \\(M=1\\), there is an open neighborhood \\(U_I\\) of \\(I_2\\) contained in \\(\\mathcal K\\) such that \\(\\mathcal P(\\Sigma)=\\{-1\\}\\) for every \\(\\Sigma\\in U_I\\), while each of \\(A_{12}\\) and \\(A_{21}\\) takes both signs across \\(\\mathcal F(\\Sigma)\\).",
    "free_symbols": [
      "kappa",
      "eta",
      "M",
      "UI",
      "K",
      "Pset",
      "Sigma",
      "A",
      "F"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:legal-fiber",
      "def:covariance-model",
      "def:polarity-set",
      "lem:affine-fiber",
      "thm:sector-characterization"
    ],
    "justification": "This proves that graph-level polarity contains information absent from either edge sign on a nondegenerate region.",
    "gap": "vanSeeventerSalehkaleybar2026 establishes positive-measure edge-sign regimes but does not exhibit joint-only identification of a feedback product.",
    "consumer": "Qualitative feedback classification remains defensible when the direction of each individual interaction is not.",
    "proof_tex": "At \\(\\Sigma=I_2\\), take \\(d=1/2\\) and \\(q=1/8\\) or \\(q=-1/8\\).  The affine formula becomes\n\\[\n A=\\begin{pmatrix}1/4&-q\\\\q&1/4\\end{pmatrix},\n \\qquad \\lVert A\\rVert_F^2=\\frac18+2\\left(\\frac18\\right)^2=\\frac5{32}<1.\n\\]\nBoth choices have strict diffusion, both edge magnitudes equal \\(1/8>1/10\\), and opposite edge signs.  Across the two choices, each individual edge takes each sign.  All these inequalities, as well as \\((1/2)I_2\\prec\\Sigma\\prec2I_2\\), persist for the same fixed \\((d,q)\\) on a sufficiently small open neighborhood of \\(I_2\\).  Hence every covariance in that neighborhood belongs to \\(\\mathcal K\\), has a negative-polarity witness, and admits both signs for each individual edge.\n\nBy the same-sign part of the sector-characterization theorem, a same-sign sector can exist only if\n\\[\n |t|\\max(s,u)\\ge2\\eta\\Delta(s+u).\n\\]\nThe reverse strict inequality holds at \\(I_2\\), since its two sides are \\(0\\) and \\(4\\eta>0\\), and it persists on a possibly smaller open neighborhood.  Both same-sign sectors are therefore empty there.  Calling the resulting intersection \\(U_I\\), we have \\(U_I\\subseteq\\mathcal K\\), \\(\\mathcal P(\\Sigma)=\\{-1\\}\\), and the two individual sign ambiguities asserted in the proposition.",
